  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "High-Pass Differential Equation",
    "lesson_core_move": "Derive the high-pass input-output differential equation from the RC circuit model by relating capacitor current, resistor current, input voltage, and output voltage.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Differential Equation",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to High-Pass Differential Equation",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the high-pass RC derivation, treat the circuit as an input-output system with input $x(t)$ and resistor-voltage output $y(t)$. The capacitor voltage is the remaining drop in the series branch, so the setup begins by naming the voltage split and the shared current.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=v_C(t)+y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That voltage-sum relation is the first cue to recognize. Once $y(t)$ is known, the capacitor voltage can be rewritten as $v_C(t)=x(t)-y(t)$, which is the form that will later let the capacitor law use the input and output directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=i_R(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the capacitor and resistor are in series, they carry the same branch current. That shared-current relation is the second cue in the derivation, and it is what lets the capacitor law and resistor law meet in one equation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=C\\,\\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_R(t)=\\frac{y(t)}{R}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the setup is complete: one voltage-sum equation, one current-equality equation, and one law for each element. The next step will substitute $v_C(t)=x(t)-y(t)$ into the capacitor law and combine it with the resistor law to reach $RC\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+y(t)=RC\\,\\frac{dx(t)}{dt}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "RC\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+y(t)=RC\\,\\frac{dx(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Differential Equation",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Writing the Input Voltage as Capacitor Voltage Plus Output Voltage",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the high-pass RC circuit, start from $x(t)=v_C(t)+y(t)$ and solve for the capacitor voltage $v_C(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the voltage-sum relation from the circuit: $x(t)=v_C(t)+y(t)$. The cue here is that the capacitor voltage is the quantity to isolate before the capacitor current law can be rewritten in terms of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=v_C(t)+y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Subtract $y(t)$ from both sides so the capacitor voltage stands alone on the left. This keeps the sign convention aligned with the series branch: the capacitor voltage is the input minus the output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "v_C(t)=x(t)-y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That isolated form is the substitution-ready result for the next step. Keep it in this exact shape so the capacitor law can be rewritten directly in terms of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Differential Equation",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Relating the Series Capacitor Current and Resistor Current",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the same high-pass RC circuit, write the capacitor current and resistor current, then set them equal because the elements are in series.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the series branch: the capacitor and resistor lie on the same current path, so they must carry the same current. Start by writing that shared-current relation explicitly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=i_R(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now write each current using the element law that matches it. The capacitor current comes from the derivative of $v_C(t)$, and the resistor current comes from the voltage across the resistor, $y(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=C\\,\\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_R(t)=\\frac{y(t)}{R}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "With both laws written in the same notation, the shared current is ready to be reused in the next step. Keep $i_C(t)=i_R(t)$ explicit and leave $v_C(t)$ unsolved here.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Differential Equation",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Substituting Output Voltage into the Capacitor Current Law",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Substitute $v_C(t)=x(t)-y(t)$ into $i_C(t)=C\\,\\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt}$ and simplify the capacitor current in terms of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the capacitor law for the shared branch current. The cue is the isolated capacitor voltage from the previous step, so replace $v_C(t)$ before doing any further algebra.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=C\\,\\frac{dv_C(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $v_C(t)=x(t)-y(t)$ directly into the derivative. Keep the subtraction intact so the sign structure stays visible through the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=C\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(x(t)-y(t)\\bigr)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Differentiate term by term. The derivative of a difference is the difference of the derivatives, so the capacitor current stays separated into the input and output rates.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "i_C(t)=C\\,\\bigl(x'(t)-y'(t)\\bigr)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the form you need next: the capacitor current is now written entirely in $x(t)$ and $y(t)$, with the substitution chain kept explicit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Differential Equation",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Rearranging into the High-Pass Input-Output Differential Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Combine $i_C(t)=C\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(x(t)-y(t)\\bigr)$ with $i_R(t)=\\frac{y(t)}{R}$ and rearrange the result into the standard high-pass input-output differential equation.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the current-balance equation from the previous step. Both branch currents are already written in $x(t)$ and $y(t)$, so the remaining job is to clear the resistor denominator and isolate the output derivative.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(x(t)-y(t)\\bigr)=\\frac{y(t)}{R}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Expand the derivative of the difference term by term. This keeps the input rate and the output rate visible before the algebraic rearrangement.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C\\,\\bigl(x'(t)-y'(t)\\bigr)=\\frac{y(t)}{R}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply through by $R$ to remove the denominator. That gives one equation with only the current scale factor and the two time-derivative terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "RC\\,\\bigl(x'(t)-y'(t)\\bigr)=y(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now move the output-derivative term to the left and keep the input-derivative term on the right. The result is the standard high-pass input-output differential equation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "RC\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}+y(t)=RC\\,\\frac{dx(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the reusable ODE form for the high-pass RC filter, and it is the form the later frequency-response lesson will start from.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
